record("ClaudeLucy-0e43ab52-c3ec-4703-90f8-4af54eb3d290-22", "{\"id\":\"ClaudeLucy-0e43ab52-c3ec-4703-90f8-4af54eb3d290-22\",\"speaker\":\"Lucy\",\"text\":\"Which base model should I be fine-tuned from?\\n\\n- Qwen3: 0.6B vs 1.7B (Recommended): Both are Apache-2.0. WebLLM has ready-made WebGPU builds for them, and your site's candle CPU runtime can run them after a small Rust change. Training needs no extra kernels. I train both, which takes minutes each on the 5090, and keep whichever passes my tests and runs acceptably on CPU: roughly 6 tokens/s for 0.6B, slower for 1.7B.\\n- Qwen3.5-0.8B (newest): Released March 2026 and likely better at 0.8B. WebLLM has ready-made builds, but candle can't run its hybrid architecture, so my CPU fallback moves to wllama (llama.cpp in WASM). Training needs transformers v5 plus two extra CUDA kernels built for the 5090.\\n- Qwen3.5-2B on wllama only: One GGUF file for both paths: wllama uses WebGPU where it exists and the CPU elsewhere. It's the best quality of the three, but it's a 1.28 GB download, it replaces candle and WebLLM for me, and the CPU path is slow unless the site sends cross-origin isolation headers.\",\"kind\":\"message\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-09-26T08:31:45Z\",\"host\":\"DESKTOP-86I05G4\",\"source\":\"claude-code\",\"session\":\"0e43ab52-c3ec-4703-90f8-4af54eb3d290\",\"channel\":\"question\",\"about\":[\"lucy\",\"slm\",\"wasm\"],\"private\":false,\"from\":null,\"kr\":null}", Message, Json).
